<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="9eafaf9">&lt;p&gt;
    Significant architectural decisions made within an organization should be documented, and typically include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Product selections
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Justification for major architectural features of projects
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Deviations from any&amp;nbsp;accepted standards&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Changes in the lifecycle defined for&amp;nbsp;introduction or modification of existing&amp;nbsp;standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Change request evaluations and approvals
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Re-use assessments
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The task begins with clearly defining the issue, followed by developing an alternate set of decisions. Then, assess
    each option for viability and document the decision using the chosen template or framework. The detail necessary to
    document each decision and whether modelling should be used to capture the existing relationships between the decisions
    will depend on the context of the project, including complexity, size of the team and stakeholder expectations.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
